{"toolCallId":"call_543a3cd7349c4cbca567a753afa20f56","title":"Read file","kind":"read","locations":[{"path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w1eb206b128/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/tasks.md","line":160}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w1eb206b128/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/tasks.md","offset":160},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"read"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_596700ed075041e5bc8abed8251b658e","title":"Read file","kind":"read","locations":[{"path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w1eb206b128/tree/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/validation/validation-rules.md","line":643,"_meta":{"cognition.ai/endLine":702}}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w1eb206b128/tree/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/validation/validation-rules.md","offset":643,"limit":60},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"read"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_5e0af4650ee5485d9df9b210fa7ee3ac","title":"Edit file","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w1eb206b128/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/plan.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"Four small edits to one bash script: a global, a parser case, a branch inside output_text, and two help blocks. Verification counts lines and diffs the default report.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag plan\"\n  - \"quiet flag implementation plan\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: plan-core | v2.3 -->\n# Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## 1. SUMMARY\n\n### Technical Context\n\n| Aspect | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Language/Stack** | Bash 3.2 or newer, POSIX shell builtins only |\n| **Framework** | None, a single script under the system-spec-kit CLI |\n| **Storage** | None |\n| **Testing** | `bash -n`, direct invocation, `wc -l`, `diff` |\n\n### Overview\n\nOne script changes: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh`. The quiet branch goes inside `output_text()` so the level label keeps one source of truth. You prove it by counting lines in both modes and diffing the default report against a captured baseline.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n## 2. QUALITY GATES\n\n### Definition of Ready\n- [x] Problem statement clear and scope documented\n- [x] Success criteria measurable\n- [x] Dependencies identified\n- [x] Baseline output captured from the unmodified script\n- [x] Precedence rule for `--json` decided in ADR-001\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] `bash -n recommend-level.sh` exits 0\n- [ ] `--quiet` prints one line and the default report matches the baseline byte for byte\n- [ ] Help text and header comment list the flag\n- [ ] `spec.md`, `plan.md` and `tasks.md` agree on the flag name and the line format\n<!-- /ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:architecture -->\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n\n### Pattern\n\nSingle script branch. The script parses arguments into globals, computes scores, then hands off to one of two renderers: `output_text()` or `output_json()`.\n\n### Key Components\n\n- **Argument parser** (`recommend-level.sh`, section 5): a `case` loop over `$1` that turns flags into globals.\n- **`QUIET` global** (section 2): a boolean beside `JSON_OUTPUT`, read by the renderer.\n- **`output_text()`** (section 4): the text renderer. It owns the `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)` string today.\n- **`output_json()`** (section 4): the JSON renderer, unchanged by this work.\n- **Header comment and `show_help()`**: the two places a user reads the option list.\n\n### Data Flow\n\n```\nargv ──► parser ──► LOC, FILES, HAS_*, QUIET, JSON_OUTPUT\n                        │\n                        ▼\n              scores ──► RECOMMENDED_LEVEL, LEVEL_NAME\n                        │\n              ┌─────────┴─────────┐\n              ▼                   ▼\n      output_text()        output_json()\n      QUIET=true?          one JSON document\n      one line : report\n```\n\n### Surfaces Touched\n\n| Surface | Current Role | Action | Verification |\n|---------|--------------|--------|--------------|\n| `output_text()` | Renders the report and the level line | update | `rg -n 'Recommended Level' recommend-level.sh` shows the string in one place |\n| `output_json()` | Renders the JSON document | unchanged | `--json` output diffs empty against the baseline |\n| `show_help()` | Prints the option list | update | `--help` lists `--quiet, -q` |\n| Header comment block | Documents options for a reader of the file | update | `sed -n '1,40p' recommend-level.sh` shows the flag |\n| `.skilled/commands/speckit/assets/speckit-plan.yaml` | Calls the script with `--json` | not a consumer of the text path | `rg -n 'recommend-level.sh' .skilled/commands/speckit/assets/` shows `--json` in both calls |\n| `.skilled/commands/speckit/assets/speckit-complete.yaml` | Calls the script with `--json` | not a consumer of the text path | same grep |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:architecture -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phases -->\n## 4. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\nFollow the ordered tasks in `tasks.md`. It owns the Setup, Implementation and Verification phase checkboxes and task state. The four steps below are the shape of the change.\n\n### Step 1: Initialize the quiet state\n\nAdd `QUIET=false` beside `JSON_OUTPUT=false` in the GLOBALS block. The script runs under `set -euo pipefail`, so a renderer that reads an unset `QUIET` aborts the run instead of printing anything.\n\nAcceptance: `QUIET` is declared before the parser and `bash -n` still exits 0.\nVerify: `rg -n '^QUIET=' recommend-level.sh` prints one line above the parser.\n\n### Step 2: Parse the flag\n\nAdd a `--quiet|-q)` case to the argument loop, setting `QUIET=true` and calling `shift`. Place it beside `--json|-j)` so the two output switches sit together.\n\nAcceptance: both spellings are accepted and neither reaches the unknown-option branch.\nVerify: `bash recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 50 --files 2` exits 0, and `bash recommend-level.sh -q --loc 50 --files 2` exits 0.\n\n### Step 3: Branch the text renderer\n\nOpen `output_text()` with a quiet guard. When `QUIET` is true, print the level line and return before the blank line, the score row, the breakdown and the phase block.\n\nAcceptance: `--quiet` prints one line, and that line is the one the default report prints.\nVerify: `bash recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 50 --files 2 | wc -l` prints 1.\n\n### Step 4: Document the flag\n\nAdd `--quiet, -q` to the header OPTIONS block and to `show_help`, with one example that uses it.\n\nAcceptance: both option lists name the flag and at least one example passes it.\nVerify: `bash recommend-level.sh --help | rg -n 'quiet'` prints two lines.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phases -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## 5. TESTING STRATEGY\n\nNo automated suite covers `recommend-level.sh` today. The verification is a set of commands a reader can rerun, captured in `tasks.md`.\n\n| Test Type | Scope | Tools |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| Syntax | The edited script parses | `bash -n` |\n| Output parity | Quiet line equals line 2 of the default report | `diff` of the quiet run against line 2 of the report |\n| Regression | Default report matches the pre-change baseline | `diff` against `scratch/baseline-default.txt` |\n| Exit codes and stderr | Invalid input in both modes | `bash recommend-level.sh --loc abc` with `$?` |\n| Help | Option list names the flag | `--help` piped to `rg` |\n\nThe baseline files live in `scratch/` and are captured from the unmodified script before the first edit.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n## 6. DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Dependency | Type | Status | Impact if Blocked |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------------|\n| `bash` 3.2 or newer | Internal | Green | The script cannot run |\n| `diff`, `wc`, `rg` for verification | Internal | Green | Verify by reading the output instead |\n| The unmodified script for a baseline | Internal | Green | Capture the baseline from `git stash` or a copy before editing |\n\nNo new package, library or service is required.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:rollback -->\n## 7. ROLLBACK PLAN\n\n- **Trigger**: The default report changes for any argument set, or a caller that passes `--json` sees different output.\n- **Procedure**: Revert the single commit that touches `recommend-level.sh`, then rerun the two baseline diffs.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n## L2: PHASE DEPENDENCIES\n\n```\nPhase 1 (Setup) ──► Phase 2 (Implementation) ──► Phase 3 (Verification)\n```\n\n| Phase | Depends On | Blocks |\n|-------|------------|--------|\n| Setup | None | Implementation |\n| Implementation | Setup | Verification |\n| Verification | Implementation | None |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:effort -->\n## L2: EFFORT ESTIMATION\n\n| Phase | Complexity | Estimated Effort |\n|-------|------------|------------------|\n| Setup | Low | 10 minutes |\n| Implementation | Low | 30 minutes |\n| Verification | Low | 20 minutes |\n| **Total** | | **1 hour (assumption)** |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:effort -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n## L2: ENHANCED ROLLBACK\n\n### Pre-deployment Checklist\n- [x] Baseline output captured (no data to back up)\n- [ ] Feature flag configured (not applicable, the flag is the switch)\n- [ ] Monitoring alerts set (not applicable, a local CLI script)\n\n### Rollback Procedure\n1. Revert the commit that edits `recommend-level.sh`.\n2. Run `bash -n` on the restored file.\n3. Rerun the default and `--json` diffs against `scratch/baseline-default.txt` and `scratch/baseline-json.json`.\n4. Tell the callers who adopted `--quiet` that the flag is gone.\n\n### Data Reversal\n- **Has data migrations?** No\n- **Reversal procedure**: N/A, the script keeps no state\n<!-- /ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n## L3: DEPENDENCY GRAPH\n\n```\n┌──────────────┐     ┌──────────────────┐     ┌────────────────┐\n│  Phase 1     │────►│  Phase 2         │────►│  Phase 3       │\n│  Setup       │     │  Implementation  │     │  Verification  │\n└──────────────┘     └──────────────────┘     └────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Dependency Matrix\n\n| Component | Depends On | Produces | Blocks |\n|-----------|------------|----------|--------|\n| Baseline capture | None | `scratch/baseline-*.txt` | Verification |\n| `QUIET` global | None | A readable boolean | Parser case, quiet branch |\n| Parser case | `QUIET` global | `QUIET=true` on demand | Quiet branch |\n| Quiet branch in `output_text()` | Parser case | The one-line output | Verification |\n| Help blocks | Flag name | Option list and examples | Documentation checks |\n| Verification | All of the above | Command evidence | Close |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n## L3: CRITICAL PATH\n\n1. **Capture the baseline** - 10 minutes - CRITICAL\n2. **Add the global and the parser case** - 15 minutes - CRITICAL\n3. **Add the quiet branch** - 15 minutes - CRITICAL\n4. **Verify both modes** - 20 minutes - CRITICAL\n\n**Total Critical Path**: 1 hour\n\n**Parallel Opportunities**:\n- The two help blocks can be edited while the verification baseline runs.\n- `spec.md` wording and the help text can be reviewed in the same pass, since both name the flag.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:milestones -->\n## L3: MILESTONES\n\n| Milestone | Description | Success Criteria | Target |\n|-----------|-------------|------------------|--------|\n| M1 | Baseline captured | `scratch/baseline-default.txt` holds 16 lines | Phase 1 |\n| M2 | Flag parsed | `--quiet` and `-q` both exit 0 | Phase 2 |\n| M3 | One-line output | `--quiet ... | wc -l` prints 1 | Phase 2 |\n| M4 | Release ready | Both diffs empty and `bash -n` exits 0 | Phase 3 |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:milestones -->\n\n---\n\n## L3: ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD\n\n### ADR-001: `--json` takes precedence over `--quiet`\n\n**Status**: Proposed\n\n**Context**: The script has one output switch today, `--json`. A quiet mode adds a second one that also decides what stdout holds. Passing both asks for a text line and a JSON document at once, and the script has to pick one.\n\n**Decision**: `--json` wins. When both flags are present the script prints the JSON document and adds no text line. The quiet branch sits inside `output_text()`, which `--json` never reaches, so the precedence falls out of the existing structure and needs no new code.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- A caller that wants a single text line never passes `--json`, and a caller that passes `--json` gets a single machine-readable document either way.\n- `--quiet --json` silently drops the quiet request. Mitigation: the help text and REQ-009 state the precedence, and a caller who needs both reads one JSON field instead.\n\n**Alternatives Rejected**:\n- Error out when both flags are passed: adds an error path and a new exit code for a combination that already has a sensible answer.\n- Let `--quiet` win and print the text line: a caller that asked for JSON would get text, which is the more surprising of the two outcomes.\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"Four small edits to one bash script: a global, a parser case, a branch inside output_text, and two help blocks. Verification counts lines and diffs the default report.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag plan\"\n  - \"quiet flag implementation plan\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: plan-core | v2.3 -->\n# Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## 1. SUMMARY\n\n### Technical Context\n\n| Aspect | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Language/Stack** | Bash 3.2 or newer, POSIX shell builtins only |\n| **Framework** | None, a single script under the system-spec-kit CLI |\n| **Storage** | None |\n| **Testing** | `bash -n`, direct invocation, `wc -l`, `diff` |\n\n### Overview\n\nOne script changes: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh`. The quiet branch goes inside `output_text()` so the level label keeps one source of truth. You prove it by counting lines in both modes and diffing the default report against a captured baseline.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n## 2. QUALITY GATES\n\n### Definition of Ready\n- [x] Problem statement clear and scope documented\n- [x] Success criteria measurable\n- [x] Dependencies identified\n- [x] Baseline output captured from the unmodified script\n- [x] Precedence rule for `--json` decided in ADR-001\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] `bash -n recommend-level.sh` exits 0\n- [ ] `--quiet` prints one line and the default report matches the baseline byte for byte\n- [ ] Help text and header comment list the flag\n- [ ] `spec.md`, `plan.md` and `tasks.md` agree on the flag name and the line format\n<!-- /ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:architecture -->\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n\n### Pattern\n\nSingle script branch. The script parses arguments into globals, computes scores, then hands off to one of two renderers: `output_text()` or `output_json()`.\n\n### Key Components\n\n- **Argument parser** (`recommend-level.sh`, section 5): a `case` loop over `$1` that turns flags into globals.\n- **`QUIET` global** (section 2): a boolean beside `JSON_OUTPUT`, read by the renderer.\n- **`output_text()`** (section 4): the text renderer. It owns the `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)` string today.\n- **`output_json()`** (section 4): the JSON renderer, unchanged by this work.\n- **Header comment and `show_help()`**: the two places a user reads the option list.\n\n### Data Flow\n\n```\nargv ──► parser ──► LOC, FILES, HAS_*, QUIET, JSON_OUTPUT\n                        │\n                        ▼\n              scores ──► RECOMMENDED_LEVEL, LEVEL_NAME\n                        │\n              ┌─────────┴─────────┐\n              ▼                   ▼\n      output_text()        output_json()\n      QUIET=true?          one JSON document\n      one line : report\n```\n\n### Surfaces Touched\n\n| Surface | Current Role | Action | Verification |\n|---------|--------------|--------|--------------|\n| `output_text()` | Renders the report and the level line | update | `rg -n 'Recommended Level' recommend-level.sh` shows the string in one place |\n| `output_json()` | Renders the JSON document | unchanged | `--json` output diffs empty against the baseline |\n| `show_help()` | Prints the option list | update | `--help` lists `--quiet, -q` |\n| Header comment block | Documents options for a reader of the file | update | `sed -n '1,40p' recommend-level.sh` shows the flag |\n| `.skilled/commands/speckit/assets/speckit-plan.yaml` | Calls the script with `--json` | not a consumer of the text path | `rg -n 'recommend-level.sh' .skilled/commands/speckit/assets/` shows `--json` in both calls |\n| `.skilled/commands/speckit/assets/speckit-complete.yaml` | Calls the script with `--json` | not a consumer of the text path | same grep |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:architecture -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phases -->\n## 4. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\nFollow the ordered tasks in `tasks.md`. It owns the Setup, Implementation and Verification phase checkboxes and task state. The four steps below are the shape of the change.\n\n### Step 1: Initialize the quiet state\n\nAdd `QUIET=false` beside `JSON_OUTPUT=false` in the GLOBALS block. The script runs under `set -euo pipefail`, so a renderer that reads an unset `QUIET` aborts the run instead of printing anything.\n\nAcceptance: `QUIET` is declared before the parser and `bash -n` still exits 0.\nVerify: `rg -n '^QUIET=' recommend-level.sh` prints one line above the parser.\n\n### Step 2: Parse the flag\n\nAdd a `--quiet|-q)` case to the argument loop, setting `QUIET=true` and calling `shift`. Place it beside `--json|-j)` so the two output switches sit together.\n\nAcceptance: both spellings are accepted and neither reaches the unknown-option branch.\nVerify: `bash recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 50 --files 2` exits 0, and `bash recommend-level.sh -q --loc 50 --files 2` exits 0.\n\n### Step 3: Branch the text renderer\n\nOpen `output_text()` with a quiet guard. When `QUIET` is true, print the level line and return before the blank line, the score row, the breakdown and the phase block.\n\nAcceptance: `--quiet` prints one line, and that line is the one the default report prints.\nVerify: `bash recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 50 --files 2 | wc -l` prints 1.\n\n### Step 4: Document the flag\n\nAdd `--quiet, -q` to the header OPTIONS block and to `show_help`, with one example that uses it.\n\nAcceptance: both option lists name the flag and at least one example passes it.\nVerify: `bash recommend-level.sh --help | rg -n 'quiet'` prints two lines.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phases -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## 5. TESTING STRATEGY\n\nNo automated suite covers `recommend-level.sh` today. The verification is a set of commands a reader can rerun, captured in `tasks.md`.\n\n| Test Type | Scope | Tools |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| Syntax | The edited script parses | `bash -n` |\n| Output parity | Quiet line equals line 2 of the default report | `diff` of the quiet run against line 2 of the report |\n| Regression | Default report matches the pre-change baseline | `diff` against `scratch/baseline-default.txt` |\n| Exit codes and stderr | Invalid input in both modes | `bash recommend-level.sh --loc abc` with `$?` |\n| Help | Option list names the flag | `--help` piped to `rg` |\n\nThe baseline files live in `scratch/` and are captured from the unmodified script before the first edit.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n## 6. DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Dependency | Type | Status | Impact if Blocked |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------------|\n| `bash` 3.2 or newer | Internal | Green | The script cannot run |\n| `diff`, `wc`, `rg` for verification | Internal | Green | Verify by reading the output instead |\n| The unmodified script for a baseline | Internal | Green | Capture the baseline from `git stash` or a copy before editing |\n\nNo new package, library or service is required.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:rollback -->\n## 7. ROLLBACK PLAN\n\n- **Trigger**: The default report changes for any argument set, or a caller that passes `--json` sees different output.\n- **Procedure**: Revert the single commit that touches `recommend-level.sh`, then rerun the two baseline diffs.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n## L2: PHASE DEPENDENCIES\n\n```\nPhase 1 (Setup) ──► Phase 2 (Implementation) ──► Phase 3 (Verification)\n```\n\n| Phase | Depends On | Blocks |\n|-------|------------|--------|\n| Setup | None | Implementation |\n| Implementation | Setup | Verification |\n| Verification | Implementation | None |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:effort -->\n## L2: EFFORT ESTIMATION\n\n| Phase | Complexity | Estimated Effort |\n|-------|------------|------------------|\n| Setup | Low | 10 minutes |\n| Implementation | Low | 30 minutes |\n| Verification | Low | 20 minutes |\n| **Total** | | **1 hour (assumption)** |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:effort -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n## L2: ENHANCED ROLLBACK\n\n### Pre-deployment Checklist\n- [x] Baseline output captured (no data to back up)\n- [ ] Feature flag configured (not applicable, the flag is the switch)\n- [ ] Monitoring alerts set (not applicable, a local CLI script)\n\n### Rollback Procedure\n1. Revert the commit that edits `recommend-level.sh`.\n2. Run `bash -n` on the restored file.\n3. Rerun the default and `--json` diffs against `scratch/baseline-default.txt` and `scratch/baseline-json.json`.\n4. Tell the callers who adopted `--quiet` that the flag is gone.\n\n### Data Reversal\n- **Has data migrations?** No\n- **Reversal procedure**: N/A, the script keeps no state\n<!-- /ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n## L3: DEPENDENCY GRAPH\n\n```\n┌──────────────┐     ┌──────────────────┐     ┌────────────────┐\n│  Phase 1     │────►│  Phase 2         │────►│  Phase 3       │\n│  Setup       │     │  Implementation  │     │  Verification  │\n└──────────────┘     └──────────────────┘     └────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Dependency Matrix\n\n| Component | Depends On | Produces | Blocks |\n|-----------|------------|----------|--------|\n| Baseline capture | None | `scratch/baseline-*.txt` | Verification |\n| `QUIET` global | None | A readable boolean | Parser case, quiet branch |\n| Parser case | `QUIET` global | `QUIET=true` on demand | Quiet branch |\n| Quiet branch in `output_text()` | Parser case | The one-line output | Verification |\n| Help blocks | Flag name | Option list and examples | Documentation checks |\n| Verification | All of the above | Command evidence | Close |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n## L3: CRITICAL PATH\n\n1. **Capture the baseline** - 10 minutes - CRITICAL\n2. **Add the global and the parser case** - 15 minutes - CRITICAL\n3. **Add the quiet branch** - 15 minutes - CRITICAL\n4. **Verify both modes** - 20 minutes - CRITICAL\n\n**Total Critical Path**: 1 hour\n\n**Parallel Opportunities**:\n- The two help blocks can be edited while the verification baseline runs.\n- `spec.md` wording and the help text can be reviewed in the same pass, since both name the flag.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:milestones -->\n## L3: MILESTONES\n\n| Milestone | Description | Success Criteria | Target |\n|-----------|-------------|------------------|--------|\n| M1 | Baseline captured | `scratch/baseline-default.txt` holds 16 lines | Phase 1 |\n| M2 | Flag parsed | `--quiet` and `-q` both exit 0 | Phase 2 |\n| M3 | One-line output | `wc -l` on the quiet run prints 1 | Phase 2 |\n| M4 | Release ready | Both diffs empty and `bash -n` exits 0 | Phase 3 |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:milestones -->\n\n---\n\n## L3: ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD\n\n### ADR-001: `--json` takes precedence over `--quiet`\n\n**Status**: Proposed\n\n**Context**: The script has one output switch today, `--json`. A quiet mode adds a second one that also decides what stdout holds. Passing both asks for a text line and a JSON document at once, and the script has to pick one.\n\n**Decision**: `--json` wins. When both flags are present the script prints the JSON document and adds no text line. The quiet branch sits inside `output_text()`, which `--json` never reaches, so the precedence falls out of the existing structure and needs no new code.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- A caller that wants a single text line never passes `--json`, and a caller that passes `--json` gets a single machine-readable document either way.\n- `--quiet --json` silently drops the quiet request. Mitigation: the help text and REQ-009 state the precedence, and a caller who needs both reads one JSON field instead.\n\n**Alternatives Rejected**:\n- Error out when both flags are passed: adds an error path and a new exit code for a combination that already has a sensible answer.\n- Let `--quiet` win and print the text line: a caller that asked for JSON would get text, which is the more surprising of the two outcomes.\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":1,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":1}}],"locations":[{"path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w1eb206b128/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/plan.md"}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w1eb206b128/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/plan.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"edit"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_62bf7f3c5bf54617952d520c1a82795f","title":"Edit file","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w1eb206b128/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"The level helper prints a 16-line report for one value. A --quiet flag prints only the recommended-level line, and the default report stays byte-identical.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\n  - \"quiet mode for level recommendation\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\nA caller that needs the recommended level from `recommend-level.sh` reads a 16-line report and pulls one value out of it. A `--quiet` flag prints only that line. The proof is a line count and a diff: one line with the flag, 16 lines without, and an unchanged report for the same inputs.\n\n**Key Decisions**: `--quiet` trims the text report and leaves the JSON document alone. The quiet line reuses the string that `output_text()` already prints, so the label keeps one source of truth.\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: `output_text()` in `recommend-level.sh` stays the only place that renders the level line.\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | P1 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-26 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n\n`recommend-level.sh` writes a 16-line text report: a blank line, the level, the score and confidence, a five-row breakdown, a blank line, and four phase lines. A script that wants the level has to pipe that through `head -2`, `tail`, `grep` or `sed` and then strip the label. Each caller re-implements the same parse, and a change to the report layout breaks all of them.\n\n### Purpose\n\nOne flag prints one line, so a caller reads the level without parsing the report.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n\n- Add a `--quiet` flag, with `-q` as its short form, to the argument parser.\n- Print only `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)` when the flag is set.\n- Leave the default text report byte-identical for the same arguments.\n- Document the flag in the script header and in `show_help`.\n- Verify both modes with commands that a reader can rerun.\n\n### Out of Scope\n\n- A JSON change: `--json` already emits one machine-readable document, so it needs no quiet variant.\n- A `--quiet` variant for `create.sh`, `validate.sh` or any other spec script.\n- Changing the scoring weights, thresholds or the level vocabulary.\n- A new test harness: no automated suite covers `recommend-level.sh` today, and this packet does not add one.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Modify | Add the `QUIET` global, the `--quiet` parser case, the quiet branch in `output_text()`, and the two help blocks |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | The argument parser accepts `--quiet` and `-q`, and both set quiet mode. |\n| REQ-002 | With `--quiet`, stdout holds exactly one line and that line reads `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)`. The line carries no leading blank line, so `wc -l` prints 1. |\n| REQ-003 | With `--quiet`, the level line is character-for-character the line the default report prints for the same arguments. |\n| REQ-004 | Without `--quiet`, stdout for a given argument set is byte-identical to the output before this change. |\n| REQ-005 | Exit codes do not change: 0 on success and 1 on invalid input, in both modes. |\n| REQ-006 | stderr does not change: `--quiet` still prints validation errors such as `ERROR: --loc must be a positive integer`. |\n| REQ-007 | `QUIET` is initialized before the parser runs, so `set -u` cannot abort the script on an unset read. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-008 | `--quiet` composes with `--no-recommend-phases` and `--phase-threshold` and still prints one line. |\n| REQ-009 | `--json` takes precedence when both `--json` and `--quiet` are passed, and the JSON document is unchanged. |\n| REQ-010 | The script header OPTIONS block and `show_help` both list `--quiet, -q` with a one-line description, and at least one example uses the flag. |\n| REQ-011 | `--quiet` changes no score: the level, score and confidence match a default run with the same arguments. |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: **Given** the default inputs `--loc 50 --files 2`, **When** the script runs with `--quiet`, **Then** it exits 0 and prints one line, `Recommended Level: 0 (Quick)`.\n- **SC-002**: **Given** the same inputs, **When** the script runs without `--quiet`, **Then** `wc -l` prints 16 and the report matches the pre-change output byte for byte.\n- **SC-003**: **Given** a Level 3 input set, **When** the script runs with `--quiet --loc 500 --files 15 --auth --api --db --architectural`, **Then** it prints `Recommended Level: 3 (Full)` and nothing else.\n- **SC-004**: **Given** both runs, **When** you diff the quiet line against the level line of the default report, **Then** the diff is empty.\n- **SC-005**: **Given** an invalid input such as `--loc abc`, **When** the script runs with `--quiet`, **Then** stderr carries the same error text as a default run and the exit code is 1.\n- **SC-006**: **Given** the edited script, **When** `bash -n recommend-level.sh` runs, **Then** it exits 0.\n- **SC-007**: **Given** `--help`, **When** the help text prints, **Then** it lists `--quiet, -q` and exits 0.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | `output_text()` stays the single renderer of the level line | The quiet line drifts from the report if the label is copied instead of reused | Put the quiet branch inside `output_text()` and reuse the existing string |\n| Dependency | `set -euo pipefail` at the top of the script | An unset `QUIET` read aborts every run under `set -u` | Initialize `QUIET=false` with the other globals |\n| Risk | A caller already scrapes the report with `head -2` and breaks when a line disappears | Medium | Keep the default report untouched, and test the byte diff |\n| Risk | The flag name collides with a future verbose mode | Low | Reserve the pair now: `--quiet` trims output, and no `--verbose` is planned |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n\n- **NFR-P01**: The flag adds no measurable time. The script stays a single bash process and the quiet path prints one `echo`.\n\n### Security\n\n- **NFR-S01**: The change reads no credential and adds no file access. The script already takes numbers and booleans only.\n\n### Reliability\n\n- **NFR-R01**: Both modes exit 0 for every valid argument set and 1 for every invalid one, with no partial output on the quiet path.\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data Boundaries\n\n- Empty input: `--quiet` with neither `--loc` nor `--files` prints `ERROR: At least --loc or --files must be provided` on stderr and exits 1, with nothing on stdout.\n- Maximum length: a very large `--loc` or `--files` value caps the score at the weight ceilings, so the quiet line still prints one line.\n\n### Error Scenarios\n\n- External service failure: none, because the script calls no network or external process.\n- Network timeout: none, for the same reason.\n\n### State Transitions\n\n- Partial completion: if `--quiet` is parsed but the input validation fails, the script exits 1 before any output, so no half line is written.\n- Session expiry: not applicable, because the script keeps no state between runs.\n\n### Argument Combinations\n\n- **Given** `--quiet --no-recommend-phases`, **When** the script runs, **Then** stdout still holds exactly one line.\n- **Given** `--quiet --json`, **When** the script runs, **Then** the JSON document prints in full and no text line is added.\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | 4/25 | Files: 1, LOC: about 12, Systems: 1 shell script |\n| Risk | 3/25 | Auth: N, API: N, Breaking: N, one shared output contract |\n| Research | 2/20 | Read one output function and count lines |\n| Multi-Agent | 1/15 | Workstreams: 1 |\n| Coordination | 2/15 | Dependencies: 0 |\n| **Total** | **12/100** | **Level 3** |\n\nThe score maps to Level 0. The declared level is 3 because the dispatch that created this packet set `--level 3`, not because the change is complex. Section 12 records the mismatch rather than hiding it.\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | A caller parses the default report and breaks when the layout changes | H | L | Keep the default report untouched and prove it with a byte diff |\n| R-002 | The quiet line drifts from the report line | M | L | Render both from the same string inside `output_text()` |\n| R-003 | An unset `QUIET` aborts the script under `set -u` | H | M | Initialize the variable with the other globals and run both modes |\n| R-004 | `--quiet` and `--json` conflict and a caller gets the wrong format | M | L | State the precedence in the spec, in the help text and in ADR-001 |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: One parseable line for a build script (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** build script that picks a documentation level, **I want** one line with the level, **so that** I read the value without parsing a 16-line report.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: The full report stays for a human reader (Priority: P1)\n\n**As an** operator running the helper by hand, **I want** the default report unchanged, **so that** the score, the confidence and the phase advice stay where they are.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-003: Stable exit codes in a pipeline (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** CI step that gates on the exit code, **I want** `--quiet` to keep the exit code and the stderr messages, **so that** a failure still fails the step.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-004: One label for maintainers (Priority: P2)\n\n**As a** maintainer of the spec scripts, **I want** the quiet line and the report line rendered from one string, **so that** a label change lands in both places at once.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- Does any caller need `--quiet` on the JSON path? Answer: no. `--json` already emits one document, and REQ-009 makes it win.\n- Should the level be 1 for a change this size? Answer: yes by the score, but the packet was created at `--level 3` by dispatch, so the level stays 3 and this row records why.\n- Should `--quiet` suppress warnings on stderr? Answer: no. stderr carries failures only, and REQ-006 keeps it.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See `plan.md` (ADR-001)\n\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"The level helper prints a 16-line report for one value. A --quiet flag prints only the recommended-level line, and the default report stays byte-identical.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\n  - \"quiet mode for level recommendation\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\nA caller that needs the recommended level from `recommend-level.sh` reads a 16-line report and pulls one value out of it. A `--quiet` flag prints only that line. The proof is a line count and a diff: one line with the flag, 16 lines without, and an unchanged report for the same inputs.\n\n**Key Decisions**: `--quiet` trims the text report and leaves the JSON document alone. The quiet line reuses the string that `output_text()` already prints, so the label keeps one source of truth.\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: `output_text()` in `recommend-level.sh` stays the only place that renders the level line.\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | P1 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-26 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n\n`recommend-level.sh` writes a 16-line text report: a blank line, the level, the score and confidence, a blank line, a breakdown heading with six rows, a blank line, and four phase lines. A script that wants the level has to pipe that through `head -2`, `tail`, `grep` or `sed` and then strip the label. Each caller re-implements the same parse, and a change to the report layout breaks all of them.\n\n### Purpose\n\nOne flag prints one line, so a caller reads the level without parsing the report.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n\n- Add a `--quiet` flag, with `-q` as its short form, to the argument parser.\n- Print only `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)` when the flag is set.\n- Leave the default text report byte-identical for the same arguments.\n- Document the flag in the script header and in `show_help`.\n- Verify both modes with commands that a reader can rerun.\n\n### Out of Scope\n\n- A JSON change: `--json` already emits one machine-readable document, so it needs no quiet variant.\n- A `--quiet` variant for `create.sh`, `validate.sh` or any other spec script.\n- Changing the scoring weights, thresholds or the level vocabulary.\n- A new test harness: no automated suite covers `recommend-level.sh` today, and this packet does not add one.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Modify | Add the `QUIET` global, the `--quiet` parser case, the quiet branch in `output_text()`, and the two help blocks |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | The argument parser accepts `--quiet` and `-q`, and both set quiet mode. |\n| REQ-002 | With `--quiet`, stdout holds exactly one line and that line reads `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)`. The line carries no leading blank line, so `wc -l` prints 1. |\n| REQ-003 | With `--quiet`, the level line is character-for-character the line the default report prints for the same arguments. |\n| REQ-004 | Without `--quiet`, stdout for a given argument set is byte-identical to the output before this change. |\n| REQ-005 | Exit codes do not change: 0 on success and 1 on invalid input, in both modes. |\n| REQ-006 | stderr does not change: `--quiet` still prints validation errors such as `ERROR: --loc must be a positive integer`. |\n| REQ-007 | `QUIET` is initialized before the parser runs, so `set -u` cannot abort the script on an unset read. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-008 | `--quiet` composes with `--no-recommend-phases` and `--phase-threshold` and still prints one line. |\n| REQ-009 | `--json` takes precedence when both `--json` and `--quiet` are passed, and the JSON document is unchanged. |\n| REQ-010 | The script header OPTIONS block and `show_help` both list `--quiet, -q` with a one-line description, and at least one example uses the flag. |\n| REQ-011 | `--quiet` changes no score: the level, score and confidence match a default run with the same arguments. |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: **Given** the default inputs `--loc 50 --files 2`, **When** the script runs with `--quiet`, **Then** it exits 0 and prints one line, `Recommended Level: 0 (Quick)`.\n- **SC-002**: **Given** the same inputs, **When** the script runs without `--quiet`, **Then** `wc -l` prints 16 and the report matches the pre-change output byte for byte.\n- **SC-003**: **Given** a Level 3 input set, **When** the script runs with `--quiet --loc 500 --files 15 --auth --api --db --architectural`, **Then** it prints `Recommended Level: 3 (Full)` and nothing else.\n- **SC-004**: **Given** both runs, **When** you diff the quiet line against the level line of the default report, **Then** the diff is empty.\n- **SC-005**: **Given** an invalid input such as `--loc abc`, **When** the script runs with `--quiet`, **Then** stderr carries the same error text as a default run and the exit code is 1.\n- **SC-006**: **Given** the edited script, **When** `bash -n recommend-level.sh` runs, **Then** it exits 0.\n- **SC-007**: **Given** `--help`, **When** the help text prints, **Then** it lists `--quiet, -q` and exits 0.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | `output_text()` stays the single renderer of the level line | The quiet line drifts from the report if the label is copied instead of reused | Put the quiet branch inside `output_text()` and reuse the existing string |\n| Dependency | `set -euo pipefail` at the top of the script | An unset `QUIET` read aborts every run under `set -u` | Initialize `QUIET=false` with the other globals |\n| Risk | A caller already scrapes the report with `head -2` and breaks when a line disappears | Medium | Keep the default report untouched, and test the byte diff |\n| Risk | The flag name collides with a future verbose mode | Low | Reserve the pair now: `--quiet` trims output, and no `--verbose` is planned |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n\n- **NFR-P01**: The flag adds no measurable time. The script stays a single bash process and the quiet path prints one `echo`.\n\n### Security\n\n- **NFR-S01**: The change reads no credential and adds no file access. The script already takes numbers and booleans only.\n\n### Reliability\n\n- **NFR-R01**: Both modes exit 0 for every valid argument set and 1 for every invalid one, with no partial output on the quiet path.\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data Boundaries\n\n- Empty input: `--quiet` with neither `--loc` nor `--files` prints `ERROR: At least --loc or --files must be provided` on stderr and exits 1, with nothing on stdout.\n- Maximum length: a very large `--loc` or `--files` value caps the score at the weight ceilings, so the quiet line still prints one line.\n\n### Error Scenarios\n\n- External service failure: none, because the script calls no network or external process.\n- Network timeout: none, for the same reason.\n\n### State Transitions\n\n- Partial completion: if `--quiet` is parsed but the input validation fails, the script exits 1 before any output, so no half line is written.\n- Session expiry: not applicable, because the script keeps no state between runs.\n\n### Argument Combinations\n\n- **Given** `--quiet --no-recommend-phases`, **When** the script runs, **Then** stdout still holds exactly one line.\n- **Given** `--quiet --json`, **When** the script runs, **Then** the JSON document prints in full and no text line is added.\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | 4/25 | Files: 1, LOC: about 12, Systems: 1 shell script |\n| Risk | 3/25 | Auth: N, API: N, Breaking: N, one shared output contract |\n| Research | 2/20 | Read one output function and count lines |\n| Multi-Agent | 1/15 | Workstreams: 1 |\n| Coordination | 2/15 | Dependencies: 0 |\n| **Total** | **12/100** | **Level 3** |\n\nThe score maps to Level 0. The declared level is 3 because the dispatch that created this packet set `--level 3`, not because the change is complex. Section 12 records the mismatch rather than hiding it.\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | A caller parses the default report and breaks when the layout changes | H | L | Keep the default report untouched and prove it with a byte diff |\n| R-002 | The quiet line drifts from the report line | M | L | Render both from the same string inside `output_text()` |\n| R-003 | An unset `QUIET` aborts the script under `set -u` | H | M | Initialize the variable with the other globals and run both modes |\n| R-004 | `--quiet` and `--json` conflict and a caller gets the wrong format | M | L | State the precedence in the spec, in the help text and in ADR-001 |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: One parseable line for a build script (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** build script that picks a documentation level, **I want** one line with the level, **so that** I read the value without parsing a 16-line report.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: The full report stays for a human reader (Priority: P1)\n\n**As an** operator running the helper by hand, **I want** the default report unchanged, **so that** the score, the confidence and the phase advice stay where they are.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-003: Stable exit codes in a pipeline (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** CI step that gates on the exit code, **I want** `--quiet` to keep the exit code and the stderr messages, **so that** a failure still fails the step.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-004: One label for maintainers (Priority: P2)\n\n**As a** maintainer of the spec scripts, **I want** the quiet line and the report line rendered from one string, **so that** a label change lands in both places at once.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- Does any caller need `--quiet` on the JSON path? Answer: no. `--json` already emits one document, and REQ-009 makes it win.\n- Should the level be 1 for a change this size? Answer: yes by the score, but the packet was created at `--level 3` by dispatch, so the level stays 3 and this row records why.\n- Should `--quiet` suppress warnings on stderr? Answer: no. stderr carries failures only, and REQ-006 keeps it.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See `plan.md` (ADR-001)\n\n\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":1,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":1}}],"locations":[{"path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w1eb206b128/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md"}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w1eb206b128/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"edit"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_63281122ace6469db68c0216978761e3","title":"Edit file","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w1eb206b128/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/plan.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"Four small edits to one bash script: a global, a parser case, a branch inside output_text, and two help blocks. Verification counts lines and diffs the default report.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag plan\"\n  - \"quiet flag implementation plan\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: plan-core | v2.3 -->\n# Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## 1. SUMMARY\n\n### Technical Context\n\n| Aspect | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Language/Stack** | Bash 3.2 or newer, POSIX shell builtins only |\n| **Framework** | None, a single script under the system-spec-kit CLI |\n| **Storage** | None |\n| **Testing** | `bash -n`, direct invocation, `wc -l`, `diff` |\n\n### Overview\n\nOne script changes: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh`. The quiet branch goes inside `output_text()` so the level label keeps one source of truth. You prove it by counting lines in both modes and diffing the default report against a captured baseline.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n## 2. QUALITY GATES\n\n### Definition of Ready\n- [x] Problem statement clear and scope documented\n- [x] Success criteria measurable\n- [x] Dependencies identified\n- [x] Baseline output captured from the unmodified script\n- [x] Precedence rule for `--json` decided in ADR-001\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] `bash -n recommend-level.sh` exits 0\n- [ ] `--quiet` prints one line and the default report matches the baseline byte for byte\n- [ ] Help text and header comment list the flag\n- [ ] `spec.md`, `plan.md` and `tasks.md` agree on the flag name and the line format\n<!-- /ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:architecture -->\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n\n### Pattern\n\nSingle script branch. The script parses arguments into globals, computes scores, then hands off to one of two renderers: `output_text()` or `output_json()`.\n\n### Key Components\n\n- **Argument parser** (`recommend-level.sh`, section 5): a `case` loop over `$1` that turns flags into globals.\n- **`QUIET` global** (section 2): a boolean beside `JSON_OUTPUT`, read by the renderer.\n- **`output_text()`** (section 4): the text renderer. It owns the `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)` string today.\n- **`output_json()`** (section 4): the JSON renderer, unchanged by this work.\n- **Header comment and `show_help()`**: the two places a user reads the option list.\n\n### Data Flow\n\n```\nargv ──► parser ──► LOC, FILES, HAS_*, QUIET, JSON_OUTPUT\n                        │\n                        ▼\n              scores ──► RECOMMENDED_LEVEL, LEVEL_NAME\n                        │\n              ┌─────────┴─────────┐\n              ▼                   ▼\n      output_text()        output_json()\n      QUIET=true?          one JSON document\n      one line : report\n```\n\n### Surfaces Touched\n\n| Surface | Current Role | Action | Verification |\n|---------|--------------|--------|--------------|\n| `output_text()` | Renders the report and the level line | update | `rg -n 'Recommended Level' recommend-level.sh` shows the string in one place |\n| `output_json()` | Renders the JSON document | unchanged | `--json` output diffs empty against the baseline |\n| `show_help()` | Prints the option list | update | `--help` lists `--quiet, -q` |\n| Header comment block | Documents options for a reader of the file | update | `sed -n '1,40p' recommend-level.sh` shows the flag |\n| `.skilled/commands/speckit/assets/speckit-plan.yaml` | Calls the script with `--json` | not a consumer of the text path | `rg -n 'recommend-level.sh' .skilled/commands/speckit/assets/` shows `--json` in both calls |\n| `.skilled/commands/speckit/assets/speckit-complete.yaml` | Calls the script with `--json` | not a consumer of the text path | same grep |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:architecture -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phases -->\n## 4. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\nFollow the ordered tasks in `tasks.md`. It owns the Setup, Implementation and Verification phase checkboxes and task state. The four steps below are the shape of the change.\n\n### Step 1: Initialize the quiet state\n\nAdd `QUIET=false` beside `JSON_OUTPUT=false` in the GLOBALS block. The script runs under `set -euo pipefail`, so a renderer that reads an unset `QUIET` aborts the run instead of printing anything.\n\nAcceptance: `QUIET` is declared before the parser and `bash -n` still exits 0.\nVerify: `rg -n '^QUIET=' recommend-level.sh` prints one line above the parser.\n\n### Step 2: Parse the flag\n\nAdd a `--quiet|-q)` case to the argument loop, setting `QUIET=true` and calling `shift`. Place it beside `--json|-j)` so the two output switches sit together.\n\nAcceptance: both spellings are accepted and neither reaches the unknown-option branch.\nVerify: `bash recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 50 --files 2` exits 0, and `bash recommend-level.sh -q --loc 50 --files 2` exits 0.\n\n### Step 3: Branch the text renderer\n\nOpen `output_text()` with a quiet guard. When `QUIET` is true, print the level line and return before the blank line, the score row, the breakdown and the phase block.\n\nAcceptance: `--quiet` prints one line, and that line is the one the default report prints.\nVerify: `bash recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 50 --files 2 | wc -l` prints 1.\n\n### Step 4: Document the flag\n\nAdd `--quiet, -q` to the header OPTIONS block and to `show_help`, with one example that uses it.\n\nAcceptance: both option lists name the flag and at least one example passes it.\nVerify: `bash recommend-level.sh --help | rg -n 'quiet'` prints two lines.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phases -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## 5. TESTING STRATEGY\n\nNo automated suite covers `recommend-level.sh` today. The verification is a set of commands a reader can rerun, captured in `tasks.md`.\n\n| Test Type | Scope | Tools |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| Syntax | The edited script parses | `bash -n` |\n| Output parity | Quiet line equals the report line | `diff <(quiet run) <(default run | sed -n '2p')` |\n| Regression | Default report matches the pre-change baseline | `diff` against `scratch/baseline-default.txt` |\n| Exit codes and stderr | Invalid input in both modes | `bash recommend-level.sh --loc abc` with `$?` |\n| Help | Option list names the flag | `--help` piped to `rg` |\n\nThe baseline files live in `scratch/` and are captured from the unmodified script before the first edit.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n## 6. DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Dependency | Type | Status | Impact if Blocked |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------------|\n| `bash` 3.2 or newer | Internal | Green | The script cannot run |\n| `diff`, `wc`, `rg` for verification | Internal | Green | Verify by reading the output instead |\n| The unmodified script for a baseline | Internal | Green | Capture the baseline from `git stash` or a copy before editing |\n\nNo new package, library or service is required.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:rollback -->\n## 7. ROLLBACK PLAN\n\n- **Trigger**: The default report changes for any argument set, or a caller that passes `--json` sees different output.\n- **Procedure**: Revert the single commit that touches `recommend-level.sh`, then rerun the two baseline diffs.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n## L2: PHASE DEPENDENCIES\n\n```\nPhase 1 (Setup) ──► Phase 2 (Implementation) ──► Phase 3 (Verification)\n```\n\n| Phase | Depends On | Blocks |\n|-------|------------|--------|\n| Setup | None | Implementation |\n| Implementation | Setup | Verification |\n| Verification | Implementation | None |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:effort -->\n## L2: EFFORT ESTIMATION\n\n| Phase | Complexity | Estimated Effort |\n|-------|------------|------------------|\n| Setup | Low | 10 minutes |\n| Implementation | Low | 30 minutes |\n| Verification | Low | 20 minutes |\n| **Total** | | **1 hour (assumption)** |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:effort -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n## L2: ENHANCED ROLLBACK\n\n### Pre-deployment Checklist\n- [x] Baseline output captured (no data to back up)\n- [ ] Feature flag configured (not applicable, the flag is the switch)\n- [ ] Monitoring alerts set (not applicable, a local CLI script)\n\n### Rollback Procedure\n1. Revert the commit that edits `recommend-level.sh`.\n2. Run `bash -n` on the restored file.\n3. Rerun the default and `--json` diffs against `scratch/baseline-default.txt` and `scratch/baseline-json.json`.\n4. Tell the callers who adopted `--quiet` that the flag is gone.\n\n### Data Reversal\n- **Has data migrations?** No\n- **Reversal procedure**: N/A, the script keeps no state\n<!-- /ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n## L3: DEPENDENCY GRAPH\n\n```\n┌──────────────┐     ┌──────────────────┐     ┌────────────────┐\n│  Phase 1     │────►│  Phase 2         │────►│  Phase 3       │\n│  Setup       │     │  Implementation  │     │  Verification  │\n└──────────────┘     └──────────────────┘     └────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Dependency Matrix\n\n| Component | Depends On | Produces | Blocks |\n|-----------|------------|----------|--------|\n| Baseline capture | None | `scratch/baseline-*.txt` | Verification |\n| `QUIET` global | None | A readable boolean | Parser case, quiet branch |\n| Parser case | `QUIET` global | `QUIET=true` on demand | Quiet branch |\n| Quiet branch in `output_text()` | Parser case | The one-line output | Verification |\n| Help blocks | Flag name | Option list and examples | Documentation checks |\n| Verification | All of the above | Command evidence | Close |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n## L3: CRITICAL PATH\n\n1. **Capture the baseline** - 10 minutes - CRITICAL\n2. **Add the global and the parser case** - 15 minutes - CRITICAL\n3. **Add the quiet branch** - 15 minutes - CRITICAL\n4. **Verify both modes** - 20 minutes - CRITICAL\n\n**Total Critical Path**: 1 hour\n\n**Parallel Opportunities**:\n- The two help blocks can be edited while the verification baseline runs.\n- `spec.md` wording and the help text can be reviewed in the same pass, since both name the flag.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:milestones -->\n## L3: MILESTONES\n\n| Milestone | Description | Success Criteria | Target |\n|-----------|-------------|------------------|--------|\n| M1 | Baseline captured | `scratch/baseline-default.txt` holds 16 lines | Phase 1 |\n| M2 | Flag parsed | `--quiet` and `-q` both exit 0 | Phase 2 |\n| M3 | One-line output | `--quiet ... | wc -l` prints 1 | Phase 2 |\n| M4 | Release ready | Both diffs empty and `bash -n` exits 0 | Phase 3 |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:milestones -->\n\n---\n\n## L3: ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD\n\n### ADR-001: `--json` takes precedence over `--quiet`\n\n**Status**: Proposed\n\n**Context**: The script has one output switch today, `--json`. A quiet mode adds a second one that also decides what stdout holds. Passing both asks for a text line and a JSON document at once, and the script has to pick one.\n\n**Decision**: `--json` wins. When both flags are present the script prints the JSON document and adds no text line. The quiet branch sits inside `output_text()`, which `--json` never reaches, so the precedence falls out of the existing structure and needs no new code.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- A caller that wants a single text line never passes `--json`, and a caller that passes `--json` gets a single machine-readable document either way.\n- `--quiet --json` silently drops the quiet request. Mitigation: the help text and REQ-009 state the precedence, and a caller who needs both reads one JSON field instead.\n\n**Alternatives Rejected**:\n- Error out when both flags are passed: adds an error path and a new exit code for a combination that already has a sensible answer.\n- Let `--quiet` win and print the text line: a caller that asked for JSON would get text, which is the more surprising of the two outcomes.\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"Four small edits to one bash script: a global, a parser case, a branch inside output_text, and two help blocks. Verification counts lines and diffs the default report.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag plan\"\n  - \"quiet flag implementation plan\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: plan-core | v2.3 -->\n# Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## 1. SUMMARY\n\n### Technical Context\n\n| Aspect | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Language/Stack** | Bash 3.2 or newer, POSIX shell builtins only |\n| **Framework** | None, a single script under the system-spec-kit CLI |\n| **Storage** | None |\n| **Testing** | `bash -n`, direct invocation, `wc -l`, `diff` |\n\n### Overview\n\nOne script changes: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh`. The quiet branch goes inside `output_text()` so the level label keeps one source of truth. You prove it by counting lines in both modes and diffing the default report against a captured baseline.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n## 2. QUALITY GATES\n\n### Definition of Ready\n- [x] Problem statement clear and scope documented\n- [x] Success criteria measurable\n- [x] Dependencies identified\n- [x] Baseline output captured from the unmodified script\n- [x] Precedence rule for `--json` decided in ADR-001\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] `bash -n recommend-level.sh` exits 0\n- [ ] `--quiet` prints one line and the default report matches the baseline byte for byte\n- [ ] Help text and header comment list the flag\n- [ ] `spec.md`, `plan.md` and `tasks.md` agree on the flag name and the line format\n<!-- /ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:architecture -->\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n\n### Pattern\n\nSingle script branch. The script parses arguments into globals, computes scores, then hands off to one of two renderers: `output_text()` or `output_json()`.\n\n### Key Components\n\n- **Argument parser** (`recommend-level.sh`, section 5): a `case` loop over `$1` that turns flags into globals.\n- **`QUIET` global** (section 2): a boolean beside `JSON_OUTPUT`, read by the renderer.\n- **`output_text()`** (section 4): the text renderer. It owns the `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)` string today.\n- **`output_json()`** (section 4): the JSON renderer, unchanged by this work.\n- **Header comment and `show_help()`**: the two places a user reads the option list.\n\n### Data Flow\n\n```\nargv ──► parser ──► LOC, FILES, HAS_*, QUIET, JSON_OUTPUT\n                        │\n                        ▼\n              scores ──► RECOMMENDED_LEVEL, LEVEL_NAME\n                        │\n              ┌─────────┴─────────┐\n              ▼                   ▼\n      output_text()        output_json()\n      QUIET=true?          one JSON document\n      one line : report\n```\n\n### Surfaces Touched\n\n| Surface | Current Role | Action | Verification |\n|---------|--------------|--------|--------------|\n| `output_text()` | Renders the report and the level line | update | `rg -n 'Recommended Level' recommend-level.sh` shows the string in one place |\n| `output_json()` | Renders the JSON document | unchanged | `--json` output diffs empty against the baseline |\n| `show_help()` | Prints the option list | update | `--help` lists `--quiet, -q` |\n| Header comment block | Documents options for a reader of the file | update | `sed -n '1,40p' recommend-level.sh` shows the flag |\n| `.skilled/commands/speckit/assets/speckit-plan.yaml` | Calls the script with `--json` | not a consumer of the text path | `rg -n 'recommend-level.sh' .skilled/commands/speckit/assets/` shows `--json` in both calls |\n| `.skilled/commands/speckit/assets/speckit-complete.yaml` | Calls the script with `--json` | not a consumer of the text path | same grep |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:architecture -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phases -->\n## 4. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\nFollow the ordered tasks in `tasks.md`. It owns the Setup, Implementation and Verification phase checkboxes and task state. The four steps below are the shape of the change.\n\n### Step 1: Initialize the quiet state\n\nAdd `QUIET=false` beside `JSON_OUTPUT=false` in the GLOBALS block. The script runs under `set -euo pipefail`, so a renderer that reads an unset `QUIET` aborts the run instead of printing anything.\n\nAcceptance: `QUIET` is declared before the parser and `bash -n` still exits 0.\nVerify: `rg -n '^QUIET=' recommend-level.sh` prints one line above the parser.\n\n### Step 2: Parse the flag\n\nAdd a `--quiet|-q)` case to the argument loop, setting `QUIET=true` and calling `shift`. Place it beside `--json|-j)` so the two output switches sit together.\n\nAcceptance: both spellings are accepted and neither reaches the unknown-option branch.\nVerify: `bash recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 50 --files 2` exits 0, and `bash recommend-level.sh -q --loc 50 --files 2` exits 0.\n\n### Step 3: Branch the text renderer\n\nOpen `output_text()` with a quiet guard. When `QUIET` is true, print the level line and return before the blank line, the score row, the breakdown and the phase block.\n\nAcceptance: `--quiet` prints one line, and that line is the one the default report prints.\nVerify: `bash recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 50 --files 2 | wc -l` prints 1.\n\n### Step 4: Document the flag\n\nAdd `--quiet, -q` to the header OPTIONS block and to `show_help`, with one example that uses it.\n\nAcceptance: both option lists name the flag and at least one example passes it.\nVerify: `bash recommend-level.sh --help | rg -n 'quiet'` prints two lines.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phases -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## 5. TESTING STRATEGY\n\nNo automated suite covers `recommend-level.sh` today. The verification is a set of commands a reader can rerun, captured in `tasks.md`.\n\n| Test Type | Scope | Tools |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| Syntax | The edited script parses | `bash -n` |\n| Output parity | Quiet line equals line 2 of the default report | `diff` of the quiet run against line 2 of the report |\n| Regression | Default report matches the pre-change baseline | `diff` against `scratch/baseline-default.txt` |\n| Exit codes and stderr | Invalid input in both modes | `bash recommend-level.sh --loc abc` with `$?` |\n| Help | Option list names the flag | `--help` piped to `rg` |\n\nThe baseline files live in `scratch/` and are captured from the unmodified script before the first edit.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n## 6. DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Dependency | Type | Status | Impact if Blocked |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------------|\n| `bash` 3.2 or newer | Internal | Green | The script cannot run |\n| `diff`, `wc`, `rg` for verification | Internal | Green | Verify by reading the output instead |\n| The unmodified script for a baseline | Internal | Green | Capture the baseline from `git stash` or a copy before editing |\n\nNo new package, library or service is required.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:rollback -->\n## 7. ROLLBACK PLAN\n\n- **Trigger**: The default report changes for any argument set, or a caller that passes `--json` sees different output.\n- **Procedure**: Revert the single commit that touches `recommend-level.sh`, then rerun the two baseline diffs.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n## L2: PHASE DEPENDENCIES\n\n```\nPhase 1 (Setup) ──► Phase 2 (Implementation) ──► Phase 3 (Verification)\n```\n\n| Phase | Depends On | Blocks |\n|-------|------------|--------|\n| Setup | None | Implementation |\n| Implementation | Setup | Verification |\n| Verification | Implementation | None |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:effort -->\n## L2: EFFORT ESTIMATION\n\n| Phase | Complexity | Estimated Effort |\n|-------|------------|------------------|\n| Setup | Low | 10 minutes |\n| Implementation | Low | 30 minutes |\n| Verification | Low | 20 minutes |\n| **Total** | | **1 hour (assumption)** |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:effort -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n## L2: ENHANCED ROLLBACK\n\n### Pre-deployment Checklist\n- [x] Baseline output captured (no data to back up)\n- [ ] Feature flag configured (not applicable, the flag is the switch)\n- [ ] Monitoring alerts set (not applicable, a local CLI script)\n\n### Rollback Procedure\n1. Revert the commit that edits `recommend-level.sh`.\n2. Run `bash -n` on the restored file.\n3. Rerun the default and `--json` diffs against `scratch/baseline-default.txt` and `scratch/baseline-json.json`.\n4. Tell the callers who adopted `--quiet` that the flag is gone.\n\n### Data Reversal\n- **Has data migrations?** No\n- **Reversal procedure**: N/A, the script keeps no state\n<!-- /ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n## L3: DEPENDENCY GRAPH\n\n```\n┌──────────────┐     ┌──────────────────┐     ┌────────────────┐\n│  Phase 1     │────►│  Phase 2         │────►│  Phase 3       │\n│  Setup       │     │  Implementation  │     │  Verification  │\n└──────────────┘     └──────────────────┘     └────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Dependency Matrix\n\n| Component | Depends On | Produces | Blocks |\n|-----------|------------|----------|--------|\n| Baseline capture | None | `scratch/baseline-*.txt` | Verification |\n| `QUIET` global | None | A readable boolean | Parser case, quiet branch |\n| Parser case | `QUIET` global | `QUIET=true` on demand | Quiet branch |\n| Quiet branch in `output_text()` | Parser case | The one-line output | Verification |\n| Help blocks | Flag name | Option list and examples | Documentation checks |\n| Verification | All of the above | Command evidence | Close |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n## L3: CRITICAL PATH\n\n1. **Capture the baseline** - 10 minutes - CRITICAL\n2. **Add the global and the parser case** - 15 minutes - CRITICAL\n3. **Add the quiet branch** - 15 minutes - CRITICAL\n4. **Verify both modes** - 20 minutes - CRITICAL\n\n**Total Critical Path**: 1 hour\n\n**Parallel Opportunities**:\n- The two help blocks can be edited while the verification baseline runs.\n- `spec.md` wording and the help text can be reviewed in the same pass, since both name the flag.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:milestones -->\n## L3: MILESTONES\n\n| Milestone | Description | Success Criteria | Target |\n|-----------|-------------|------------------|--------|\n| M1 | Baseline captured | `scratch/baseline-default.txt` holds 16 lines | Phase 1 |\n| M2 | Flag parsed | `--quiet` and `-q` both exit 0 | Phase 2 |\n| M3 | One-line output | `--quiet ... | wc -l` prints 1 | Phase 2 |\n| M4 | Release ready | Both diffs empty and `bash -n` exits 0 | Phase 3 |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:milestones -->\n\n---\n\n## L3: ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD\n\n### ADR-001: `--json` takes precedence over `--quiet`\n\n**Status**: Proposed\n\n**Context**: The script has one output switch today, `--json`. A quiet mode adds a second one that also decides what stdout holds. Passing both asks for a text line and a JSON document at once, and the script has to pick one.\n\n**Decision**: `--json` wins. When both flags are present the script prints the JSON document and adds no text line. The quiet branch sits inside `output_text()`, which `--json` never reaches, so the precedence falls out of the existing structure and needs no new code.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- A caller that wants a single text line never passes `--json`, and a caller that passes `--json` gets a single machine-readable document either way.\n- `--quiet --json` silently drops the quiet request. Mitigation: the help text and REQ-009 state the precedence, and a caller who needs both reads one JSON field instead.\n\n**Alternatives Rejected**:\n- Error out when both flags are passed: adds an error path and a new exit code for a combination that already has a sensible answer.\n- Let `--quiet` win and print the text line: a caller that asked for JSON would get text, which is the more surprising of the two outcomes.\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":1,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":1}}],"locations":[{"path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w1eb206b128/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/plan.md"}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w1eb206b128/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/plan.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"edit"}}
